The article uses this work to support the idea that costs for reaching a given level of performance keep halving over time, a claim it applies specifically to GPT-3 to argue that even an expensive model will get cheaper as algorithms improve. The abstract on arxiv.org and the matching paper on the OpenAI site say that the compute needed to train a classifier to match AlexNet on ImageNet fell by a factor of 44 between 2012 and 2019, an efficiency doubling roughly every 16 months over that stretch, and that this rate of improvement actually outpaced Moore's law style hardware gains over the same years. A details page from the same project gives similar doubling times for other tasks, such as machine translation, image classification, and game-playing systems like AlphaGo Zero and OpenAI Five, some closer to four to six months. So the specific number the article leans on, costs halving roughly every 16 months, matches what these pages report, though it comes from image-recognition benchmarks rather than from language models like GPT-3 itself.

