Beyond that one number, the work is a broader attempt to measure something usually treated as fuzzy, how much better algorithms get over time, separately from bigger chips or bigger training runs. That fits the article's larger argument that raw scale is not the only force at work and that efficiency keeps compounding on top of it, making both bigger and cheaper models likely. Since its measurements come from vision and game-playing systems rather than large text models, applying its doubling-time estimate to GPT-3 style language models, as the article does more than once, stretches the work a step beyond what it directly measured.
